Commit:     hkjang <gagagiga@naver.com>
CommitDate: Tue Oct 6 16:14:50 2026 +0900

    perf(sqlsafe): deduplicate violation reasons through a membership index

    Violation reason strings embed identifiers lifted straight out of the
    query -- function names, cast target types, string literal types -- so the
    number of distinct reasons a statement can produce is bounded only by the
    query length. appendUnique compared every new reason against every reason
    already collected, which is quadratic in that count, and AnalyzeDialect's
    merge loop then paid the same cost a second time moving those reasons onto
    the statement's own list.

    runtimeapi.(*API).validateSQL accepts up to 1<<20 bytes from any
    authenticated principal with no database privilege and no approval. At that
    limit a query of distinct stored function calls pinned a core for 13.5s
    (postgres) and 13.2s (oracle) per request.

    Accumulate reasons in a reasonSet that keeps an order-preserving slice as
    the only source of order and consults a lazily built map for membership
    only. Both dialect gates and the merge loop use it; the merge seeds the set
    from the statement reasons so their order and the deduplication against
    them are unchanged. Below reasonSetIndexThreshold reasons the original
    linear scan still runs, so ordinary queries never allocate the map.

    appendUnique stays at the Analyze call sites, where reason cardinality is
    bounded by the keyword tables rather than by the query.

    Measured at the 1 MiB request limit (96,332 distinct names): postgres
    13.5s -> 125ms, oracle 13.2s -> 105ms. Verdicts are unchanged: 520
    verdicts over 130 realistic queries x 4 dialect spellings compare
    byte-identical against the previous implementation, as do 760,000
    differential comparisons over random and generated projections. Forcing
    the threshold high enough to disable the index fails only the three scale
    tests and leaves every correctness test green.

