# Offline installation

## Connected build/release workstation

The official release asset is named `qurio-vX.Y.Z.tar.gz` and contains a Docker
image tagged `qurio:vX.Y.Z`. Build it with:

```bash
./scripts/release.sh
```

The release script verifies the completed archive, not only the build-stage
image: it runs `gzip -t`, removes the original tag from the test namespace,
loads the archive back into Docker, checks the exact tag and `linux/amd64`
platform, confirms that no Oracle Instant Client is present, and starts the
reloaded image against an isolated PostgreSQL container. Readiness must pass
with only the four application configuration variables before an archive is
eligible for release.

Copy the archive, `compose.yml`, `.env.example`, and this guide through the
approved transfer process. Verify the SHA-256 value printed in the GitHub
release notes before importing it into the offline network. The GitHub release
intentionally attaches only the `.tar.gz` image archive; a local build also
creates a sidecar checksum for internal transfer controls.

## Metadata PostgreSQL prerequisite

Create the PostgreSQL database and its dedicated login/owner before starting
Qurio, then put that connection string in `POSTGRES_DSN`. The database itself
must exist; its schema may be completely empty. A fresh installation does not
require importing any SmartSQL SQL, schema, or data.

Prefer a non-superuser database owner. It must be able to create objects in the
database and `public` schema, install the trusted `pgcrypto` extension, and
own/manage the objects Qurio creates. An equivalently privileged dedicated role
is also supported. Ensure the PostgreSQL server has `pgcrypto` available before
moving into the offline network.

